{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 12, "model_name": "nvidia/llama-3.3-nemotron-super-49b-v1", "content": "Oregon's pioneering drug decriminalization policy will end with the introduction of a new law, HB4002, which recriminalizes possession of small amounts of hard drugs like fentanyl and heroin. Those caught can choose between charges or treatment, including a behavioral health program and a deflection initiative to avoid fines. Possession for personal use will be a misdemeanor, punishable by up to six months in jail.\n\n***\n\nThe new law aims to enhance police ability to address public drug use and imposes stricter penalties for selling drugs near areas like parks. It encourages counties to establish treatment alternatives, though this is not mandatory. Supporters argue this flexibility allows counties to tailor programs to their resources, while opponents worry it may create inconsistent policies across different regions.\n\n***\n\nEligibility for deflection into treatment is tightly defined, requiring no outstanding charges, warrants, or history of violent behavior, as well as medical stability. If eligible, police will contact a county dispatch line to connect individuals with a mobile behavioral health team, which must make contact within 30 minutes or the person will be jailed due to logistical constraints.\n\n***\n\nThe shift in policy underscores the challenges in balancing public safety with addiction treatment. As Oregon navigates this change, the impact on those struggling with addiction and the broader societal effects will be closely watched. \nP.P.S. As individuals of all age groups in this country navigate the complexities of substance use, they must also find time to eat and engage in everyday activities like shopping, all while perhaps wondering how this policy affects the tongue of public discourse.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob